#X text 675 22 This part of the patch introduces a simple volume control and simple ways to change the frequency of an [osc~] object. Remember that the audible range of frequencies to human hearing is approximately 20Hz to 20KHz. Computer speakers may not produce audible sounds at the extremes of those ranges. ALSO \, TAKE NOTE that the volume control object \, [/~ 2] has a creation argument of 2 This is because there are two oscillators and the output of those oscillators is between 0 and 1 If they both produce values of 1 at the same time or anything that when added together \, is greater than 1 \, it is outside the range that can be produced without distorting the output. THE TOP horizontal slider has a range of numbers from 0 to 20 \, 0 The bottom horizontal slider has a range from 0 to 127 The bottom one is there because the resolution of the top one does not give easy access to the bottom range of numbers at its extreme.;
#X obj 537 309 hsl 162 19 0 1 0 0 empty empty 0\ to\ 1 -2 -10 0 12 #fcfcfc #000000 #000000 0 1;
#X floatatom 536 348 14 0 0 0 - - - 0;
#X text 715 279 This slider controls the volume of the patch and it does so for the whole thing because the output of it feeds into the [/~ 2] object which divides its output by 2 before it gets to the [dac~]. The number box below the 0 to 1 slider is for debugging but can also be useful if you want to see the level of the number itself and not use just your ears. Sometimes \, this can be useful for more than what is represented by just the number in the number box. Sometimes this is useful to provide context for the other numbers in the patch so you understand what is happening there as well. Once a patch is completed \, it is up to you if you want to remove the unecessary parts to clean up. Sometimes things can look cluttered. One useful pair of objects I have come across in my own explorations are [send] and [receive] \, often abbreviated [s] and [r]. You can see their use in a more recent patch or two before this one.;
